{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.85,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["J3", "U25"],
      "pins": ["J3.A7", "J3.B7", "U25.4"],
      "nets": ["USBC_D_N"],
      "observed": "The net named USBC_D_N contains the USB-C receptacle D- pins (J3 A7 and B7) and U25 pin 4 (I/O2), but it is also the board ground net: it contains every Module1 GND pin, the ground side of essentially every decoupling capacitor (C1.2, C2.2, C3.2, ...), the ground pins of U1/U2/U8/U9/U10/U11/U13/U14/U15/U17/U18/U19/U20/U21/U22/U23/U24/U25/U26/U27/U28/U29/U30/U31/U32/U33/U34/U35/U36, the shield of J3 (S1), etc. So the USB-C D- line is tied directly to GND.",
      "expected": "USBC_D_N should be an isolated net carrying only the USB-C D- line (J3 A7/B7 and the U25 I/O2 pin that faces the connector), with no connection to the board ground net. The D+ side (net USBC_D_P) is correctly kept separate, so the D- side should be too.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"203\") (name \"USBC_D_N\") (class \"Default\") ... (node (ref \"J3\") (pin \"A7\") (pinfunction \"D-\") (pintype \"bidirectional\")) ... (node (ref \"J3\") (pin \"B7\") (pinfunction \"D-\") (pintype \"bidirectional\")) ... (node (ref \"U25\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"I/O2\") (pintype \"passive\")) ... (node (ref \"Module1\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"GND\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ..."},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"204\") (name \"USBC_D_P\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"J3\") (pin \"A6\") (pinfunction \"D+\") (pintype \"bidirectional\")) (node (ref \"J3\") (pin \"B6\") (pinfunction \"D+\") (pintype \"bidirectional\")) (node (ref \"U25\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"I/O1\") (pintype \"passive\")))"}
      ],
      "impact": "The USB-C D- conductor is shorted to ground, so the USB-C data pair is broken (D+ still routes through U25/U24, but D- is clamped to 0 V). USB-C data communication (and any USB device/host function that depends on the D- line) will not work; only VBUS power delivery is unaffected.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 is placed across the rectified Ethernet-power rails (VOUTP to VOUTN). Its value field is \"24k\" and the SPICE deck models it as 24k, but its description field reads \"... ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor ...\" and its MPN is FRC0805J240TSD (a 24 Ω, 5 % part). The value, description and MPN do not agree.",
      "expected": "The value, description and MPN of a two-terminal passive should all describe the same part. For a bleeder/divider element across a rail that can reach tens of volts, 24 kΩ is the plausible value, so the description/MPN (24 Ω) are the inconsistent fields.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\") ... (field (name \"Description\") \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\"))"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the BOM/assembly follows the MPN or description, a 24 Ω resistor is fitted across VOUTP/VOUTN instead of 24 kΩ, which would heavily load (and likely damage) the Ethernet-derived power rail; if the value field is followed, the board is correct but the BOM metadata is wrong.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain a real anomaly: the USB-C D- line (net USBC_D_N, J3 A7/B7 and U25 pin 4) is merged with the board ground net, shorting USB-C D- to GND. A secondary metadata inconsistency exists on R36 (value 24k vs description/MPN 24Ω)."
}

